  "hints": [
    "start by defining dp[i] as the length of the longest increasing subsequence that ends exactly at index i (not just among the first i elements)",
    "for a fixed i, which earlier indices j could dp[i] extend? what condition on nums[j] and nums[i] has to hold?",
    "dp[i] = 1 + max(dp[j]) over every j < i where nums[j] < nums[i], or just 1 if no such j exists - the answer is the max over all dp[i]",
    "once the O(n^2) version works: instead of tracking dp per index, maintain a separate array of 'the smallest tail value achievable for an increasing subsequence of each length' and binary search it for where nums[i] belongs - that array's final length is the answer"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "confusing 'longest increasing subsequence' with 'longest increasing contiguous run', which is a much simpler O(n) problem",
    "using a non-strict comparison (allowing equal values to extend the subsequence) when the problem requires strictly increasing",
    "in the O(n log n) version, mistaking the tails array for an actual subsequence - it tracks the best possible tail per length, not a real answer sequence"
  ],
  "content": {
